## Domain 1: Agents and Workflows (Q1.1–Q1.9)

### Q1.1 · Agent Architecture
An invoice-approval process matches PO line items automatically 95% of the time. The  
remaining 5% need judgment (partial shipments, unusual unit pricing) that a fixed sequence  
can't anticipate in advance. The team is deciding between a pure fixed workflow, a pure  
autonomous agent, and a hybrid.

A) A pure fixed workflow, since automating 95% of cases already delivers most of the value  
   and the remaining 5% can simply be rejected outright.
B) A hybrid: a fixed workflow for the deterministic matching steps, with a conditional  
   branch that hands off to agentic reasoning (or a human) specifically for the unmatched 5%  
   — full autonomy isn't needed for the 95% that's already fully specified, and a rigid  
   workflow can't handle the judgment cases at all.
C) A pure autonomous agent for every invoice, since judgment might be needed on any given  
   invoice and a workflow can't know in advance which ones.
D) A manager/supervisor hierarchy with a dedicated subagent for every possible exception  
   type, since supervisors are the correct pattern whenever any branching exists.

### Q1.2 · Agent Architecture
A contracts-drafting agent generates a first draft, then a second agent reviews it  
specifically for compliance-sensitive language before it's shown to the user. For a  
low-stakes internal memo template with no compliance exposure, the same  
generator-then-reviewer pattern is applied unconditionally.

A) The generator-and-reviewer (critic) pattern is a legitimate supervisor-style use of a  
   subagent when there's real compliance risk to catch; applying it unconditionally to  
   low-stakes content that carries no such risk adds cost and latency without a  
   corresponding benefit — the pattern should be reserved for content where the review  
   actually matters.
B) The pattern is never appropriate, since a single agent should always be trusted to  
   self-review its own output without a separate subagent.
C) The pattern is always appropriate regardless of stakes, since a second independent pass  
   can only improve quality.
D) The fix is to merge the generator and reviewer into one agent with a longer system prompt  
   describing both roles, since one larger prompt is always more efficient than two agents.

### Q1.3 · Agent Architecture
A two-step task — fetch a record, then format it into a templated summary — is currently  
implemented by delegating the entire task to a subagent via `Task`, purely because "that's  
how we do agentic work here." Elsewhere in the same codebase, a genuinely open-ended  
multi-source investigation is implemented as plain sequential tool calls in the main agent.

A) Both are implemented correctly, since subagent delegation and plain tool calls are  
   stylistic choices with no material difference in outcome.
B) Both are backwards: the two-step fetch-and-format task doesn't need isolated context or  
   parallelism and pays subagent overhead for nothing, while the open-ended investigation is  
   exactly the kind of expendable-context, multi-round work that benefits from delegation.
C) Both are correct as long as the same model tier is used in each case.
D) The fetch-and-format task is correctly delegated, since any task touching more than one  
   tool should always go through a subagent regardless of complexity.

### Q1.4 · Agent Construction with Claude
A team wants an unconditional audit trail of every tool call an agent makes — including  
calls the agent doesn't happen to mention in its final summary to the user — for a  
compliance requirement that the record be complete regardless of what the model chooses to  
report.

A) Ask the model, via the system prompt, to always list every tool it called at the end of  
   its response, and parse that list for the audit log.
B) Implement a hook that fires on every tool-call event (e.g., a `PostToolUse` hook) and  
   writes the audit record directly from the harness, independent of anything the model does  
   or doesn't mention in its own output.
C) Increase the model's context window so it's less likely to omit a tool call from its  
   summary.
D) Lower the temperature so the model's self-reported summaries become more consistent and  
   complete.

### Q1.5 · Agent Construction with Claude
A parent session delegates two independent, self-contained research questions to subagents  
via `context: fork`, expecting each to explore in isolation and return a summary. Partway  
through, an engineer updates a shared reference file the parent session is using and expects  
both already-forked subagents to pick up the change automatically without any explicit  
re-fetch.

A) Correct — forked children remain live-linked to the parent's context, so any update to  
   the parent after the fork propagates automatically to already-forked children.
B) Incorrect — a fork's context is independent of the parent from the moment of the fork  
   forward; an update made to the parent afterward does not retroactively reach children  
   that already forked, so anything they need to pick up must be explicitly passed or  
   re-fetched.
C) Correct, but only for updates made within the same API call as the fork itself.
D) Incorrect, because `context: fork` doesn't create independent context at all — it's  
   functionally identical to sharing the same context object by reference.

### Q1.6 · Agent Construction with Claude
A healthcare company is bound by strict data-residency rules requiring that patient data  
never leave infrastructure the company directly controls, and needs auditable, low-level  
visibility into exactly how model calls are routed and logged — beyond what a fully managed  
offering exposes.

A) An Anthropic-hosted managed deployment, since it's always the faster path to production  
   regardless of data-residency constraints.
B) A self-hosted deployment (e.g., via a cloud platform with the required residency  
   guarantees), trading some deployment speed and operational simplicity for the direct  
   infrastructure control and auditability the compliance requirement demands.
C) A managed deployment is still correct here, since compliance requirements are satisfied  
   automatically by any Anthropic-hosted offering regardless of data residency terms.
D) Neither option is viable — regulated healthcare data can never be processed by an LLM  
   under any deployment model.

### Q1.7 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A team adopts a graph-based agentic framework (with cycles, conditional edges, and shared  
state across nodes) for a task that is, in practice, three tool calls executed in a fixed,  
unchanging order every single time, with no branching and no need to revisit an earlier  
step.

A) The graph framework is a reasonable default for any agentic work, since more structure is  
   never a disadvantage regardless of the task's actual shape.
B) The graph framework is overkill here: a task with fixed order, no cycles, and no  
   conditional branching doesn't need the state-graph machinery a framework like this exists  
   to manage — a simple explicit chain of calls fits the task with less overhead to build  
   and maintain.
C) The graph framework should be replaced with a fully autonomous single agent instead,  
   since removing structure entirely is the correct response to over-engineering.
D) The choice of framework has no bearing on runtime behavior, since frameworks only affect  
   build-time tooling, not execution.

### Q1.8 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
Within a single active coding session, the agent needs to track "which file is currently  
being edited" for the next few turns. Separately, the same assistant is expected to recall,  
weeks later in a brand-new session, that this particular user always wants commit messages  
in a specific format.

A) Both belong in the same place: an external memory store queried at the start of every  
   session, since all state should be centralized regardless of how long it needs to  
   persist.
B) The current-file tracking is short-term, session-scoped state that belongs in the active  
   context; the commit-message preference is durable, cross-session state that belongs in  
   external memory, loaded selectively when a new session starts — different lifetimes call  
   for different mechanisms.
C) Both belong in the system prompt, hardcoded permanently, since system prompts are the  
   correct home for all state regardless of how often it changes.
D) Neither needs explicit handling, since the model will infer both from general  
   conversational context without any storage mechanism.

### Q1.9 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks *(select TWO)*
Which two statements about adopting an agentic abstraction framework (e.g., a graph-based  
orchestration library) are accurate?

A) A framework can meaningfully reduce the amount of custom harness code needed for common  
   patterns like state handling and multi-step orchestration, for tasks that actually have  
   that shape.
B) Adopting a framework guarantees the resulting agent's outputs will be deterministic  
   across runs, since the framework enforces a fixed execution graph.
C) A framework's orchestration benefits come with real tradeoffs — added abstraction, a  
   learning curve, and sometimes less direct control over the underlying tool-use loop —  
   that should be weighed against a task's actual complexity rather than adopted by default.
D) Using a well-known framework eliminates the need for the team's own evaluation suite,  
   since the framework's own tests already validate any application built on it.
E) Framework adoption automatically fine-tunes the underlying model to the specific task,  
   improving accuracy over time without additional effort.

