## Domain 2: Applications and Integration (Q2.1–Q2.19)

### Q2.1 · Understanding Requirements
A solution architecture document states: "the assistant shall summarize each uploaded  
document into three bullet points." A separate line states: "summaries must complete within  
4 seconds for documents up to 50 pages, at up to 100 concurrent uploads." A third line  
states: "the business wants to cut document-review time company-wide by 30% this year."

A) All three are functional requirements, since all three describe what the system is  
   expected to do in some sense.
B) The first is functional (a specific behavior), the second is an infrastructure  
   requirement (a latency/capacity constraint), and the third is a business goal the first  
   two requirements exist to help achieve — not a requirement itself.
C) The second and third are both infrastructure requirements, since both relate to  
   system-wide performance.
D) The first and third are both business goals, since summarization is ultimately a  
   business-driven feature.

### Q2.2 · Understanding Requirements
A requirements document specifies "the system must never expose one tenant's documents to  
another tenant" for a multi-tenant SaaS product. A junior engineer classifies this as a  
functional requirement, reasoning "it describes what the system does — it keeps data  
separated."

A) Correct — any statement describing observable system behavior is, by definition, a  
   functional requirement.
B) More accurately a non-functional/infrastructure requirement: it's a cross-cutting  
   quality/security constraint the system must satisfy across all functionality, rather than  
   a specific feature behavior like "summarize this document" — the distinction matters  
   because these constraints typically require architecture-level enforcement, not a single  
   feature's logic.
C) It isn't a requirement of any kind, since data isolation is assumed by default in any  
   software system and doesn't need to be stated.
D) It's a business goal rather than a requirement, since data isolation ultimately serves  
   customer trust.

### Q2.3 · Systems Life Cycle
A team is in the earliest phase of a new Claude-powered project: interviewing stakeholders,  
documenting what the assistant must and must not do, and identifying the infrastructure  
constraints (latency, concurrency, data residency) the eventual system must satisfy — no  
code has been written yet.

A) Operations and maintenance — gathering constraints is an ongoing activity throughout a  
   system's life, including after launch.
B) Requirements analysis — the defining activity of this phase is precisely eliciting and  
   documenting functional and infrastructure requirements before design or implementation  
   begins.
C) Implementation — documenting requirements is a preparatory implementation task.
D) System design — requirements gathering and system design are the same activity under a  
   different name.

### Q2.4 · Systems Life Cycle
A team has finished requirements analysis for a new agent and is now deciding the concrete  
architecture: which components will be a fixed workflow versus an agentic loop, which tools  
are needed, how memory will be scoped, and which model tier fits each sub-task — before any  
of it is built.

A) This is implementation, since architecture decisions directly determine what gets coded.
B) This is system design — translating validated requirements into a concrete technical  
   architecture (workflow/agent split, tool set, memory scope, model selection) is the  
   defining activity of the design phase, distinct from the requirements analysis that  
   preceded it and the implementation that follows.
C) This is operations and maintenance, since model tier selection is a cost-optimization  
   concern typically handled post-launch.
D) This phase doesn't exist separately in a systems life cycle — requirements analysis flows  
   directly into implementation with no intermediate design step.

### Q2.5 · Claude API Mechanics
A team invoking Claude through a third-party cloud platform (rather than Anthropic's  
first-party API) needs to know which Claude-specific features they should verify are  
available on that platform before committing to it, since not every capability is guaranteed  
to have identical availability or access patterns across every provider at every point in  
time.

A) No verification is needed — every third-party cloud platform offers full feature parity  
   with the first-party API at all times, by contractual guarantee.
B) Verify feature availability (e.g., specific model snapshots, extended thinking support,  
   batch processing, prompt caching) against that specific platform's current documentation  
   before committing, since access patterns and feature rollout timing can differ across  
   providers.
C) Verification is unnecessary because third-party platforms only ever host a fixed,  
   unchanging feature set that never varies from the first-party API's launch-day  
   capabilities.
D) Feature availability only matters for the first-party API; third-party platforms are  
   exempt from needing any of Claude's core capabilities to be present.

### Q2.6 · Claude API Mechanics
A document-QA feature needs to both look at a scanned form (image input) and reason  
carefully through multiple cross-referenced fields to answer a compound question. A  
separate, simpler feature just needs to classify a single short text string into one of  
three categories with minimal latency.

A) Enable extended/adaptive thinking and supply the image as a content block for the  
   document-QA feature; skip thinking entirely for the simple classification feature, where  
   added reasoning would only increase latency without improving a task that's already  
   straightforward.
B) Enable extended thinking for both features uniformly, since more reasoning always  
   improves output quality regardless of task complexity.
C) Supply the scanned form as raw text extracted via a separate OCR step, since Claude  
   cannot process image content natively.
D) Disable thinking for both features, since thinking and vision inputs are mutually  
   exclusive within the same account.

### Q2.7 · Claude API Mechanics
A streaming chat application occasionally drops the connection mid-response due to a flaky  
client network, leaving a partial response the user never sees completed. The team's current  
handling: silently discard the partial output and make the user re-ask the same question  
from scratch every time this happens.

A) This is acceptable, since streaming inherently cannot be made resilient to connection  
   drops.
B) Implement reconnection/retry handling that can resume or restart the specific interrupted  
   request cleanly (e.g., re-issuing the call and discarding the stale partial state) rather  
   than silently losing all progress and forcing the user to notice and manually re-ask.
C) Switch the application to the Message Batches API to eliminate the possibility of a  
   mid-response disconnect.
D) Increase `max_tokens` so responses complete faster and are less likely to be interrupted.

### Q2.8 · Claude API Mechanics
A backend service reads Claude's responses purely via the standard request/response Messages  
API pattern, one call per user turn. The team is evaluating whether adopting a persistent  
connection protocol would improve anything for this specific access pattern, where each user  
turn is independent and there's no need for the server to push unsolicited updates to the  
client between turns.

A) A persistent connection protocol would meaningfully improve this specific pattern, since  
   persistent connections are strictly better than request/response for any application  
   involving Claude.
B) For this access pattern — one request per turn, no server-initiated pushes needed — a  
   standard request/response call is already the right fit; a persistent connection earns  
   its complexity for patterns needing continuous bidirectional or server-pushed updates,  
   which this workload doesn't have.
C) The Messages API only supports request/response patterns and can never be used within an  
   architecture that also uses persistent connections elsewhere in the same application.
D) This decision has no bearing on the application's behavior, since transport choice is  
   invisible to the model regardless of pattern.

### Q2.9 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team designs a new internal REST endpoint that wraps a Claude-powered feature. The  
endpoint currently returns `200 OK` for every request, including ones where the underlying  
Claude call failed or where input validation rejected the request, with error details buried  
in a text field the client has to string-match to detect failure.

A) This is acceptable REST design, since HTTP status codes are only a legacy convention with  
   no practical benefit over consistent 200 responses.
B) Use HTTP status codes to reflect actual outcomes (4xx for client/validation errors, 5xx  
   for upstream/service failures, 200 only for genuine success), so clients can rely on  
   structured status rather than parsing free text to determine whether a call succeeded.
C) Return `200 OK` for every response, but add a comment in the API documentation explaining  
   that clients must always check the text field, since documentation is sufficient to  
   convey this reliably.
D) Switch the endpoint from REST to a websocket-based protocol, since status-code semantics  
   are a REST-specific limitation.

### Q2.10 · Software Engineering Foundations
A tool's input schema returns JSON where a new optional field was recently added. A  
downstream consumer written before the schema change breaks because it uses strict schema  
validation that rejects any field not explicitly declared, treating the new optional field  
as an unexpected, invalid property.

A) This is expected and acceptable, since any schema change should always require  
   simultaneously updating every consumer in lockstep.
B) Design schemas and their consumers for additive, backward-compatible evolution — new  
   optional fields shouldn't break existing consumers; strict validation that rejects  
   unknown fields needs to be paired with a deliberate versioning/compatibility policy, not  
   applied by default to every field ever added.
C) Never add new fields to a schema once any consumer exists, since schemas should be  
   permanently frozen after first use.
D) Remove all validation from the downstream consumer entirely, since any validation at all  
   risks breaking on future changes.

### Q2.11 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team wants a lightweight, low-risk way to verify that AI-generated code changes meet the  
same bar as human-written changes before merging, without slowing down every trivial change  
to a crawl.

A) Apply the same code review process to AI-generated changes as to human-written changes,  
   scaled to the actual risk and size of the change — a trivial one-line fix gets a lighter  
   review than a multi-file architectural change, regardless of who or what authored it.
B) Skip review entirely for AI-generated changes under some size threshold, since  
   sufficiently small changes are inherently safe regardless of origin.
C) Require every AI-generated change, regardless of size, to go through the same heavyweight  
   review as a major refactor, since AI-authored code always carries more risk than  
   human-authored code of equivalent size.
D) Disable CI checks specifically for AI-generated pull requests to reduce review friction,  
   relying on manual inspection alone.

### Q2.12 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team plans a small-scale refactor (renaming a handful of internal functions with no  
behavior change) and, separately, a large-scale refactor (restructuring how an entire  
billing module composes its Claude tool calls). They're using the identical process — a  
single large PR, minimal test additions, and one reviewer — for both.

A) This is fine, since refactor scale doesn't meaningfully change the appropriate process.
B) Scale the process to the refactor: the small-scale rename can reasonably stay  
   lightweight, but the large-scale restructuring benefits from the practices used for  
   higher-risk change — incremental steps, characterization tests, and broader review —  
   precisely because its blast radius and behavioral risk are much larger.
C) Both refactors should use the heaviest possible process (multiple reviewers, extensive  
   new test suites, incremental rollout) regardless of scale, since refactoring is  
   inherently risky at any size.
D) Neither refactor needs tests, since refactors by definition don't change behavior and  
   therefore can't introduce regressions.

### Q2.13 · Claude Application Design
An application exposes the exact same underlying capability through Claude Code (for  
internal engineers), a claude.ai-embedded widget (for external customers), and a headless  
API integration (for a partner's backend). The team assumes identical prompt text will  
produce identical behavior across all three, since "it's the same model."

A) Correct — the underlying model is the same across all interfaces, so identical prompt  
   text always produces identical behavior regardless of surface.
B) Incorrect — each interface has its own surrounding behaviors (available tools, default  
   system-level instructions, product-level constraints on claude.ai vs. the blank-slate API  
   vs. Claude Code's own operating context) that shape how identical prompt text is actually  
   interpreted; surface-specific adaptation is expected, not a bug.
C) Correct, but only because all three surfaces happen to share the exact same tool  
   configuration in this scenario.
D) Incorrect, but only because claude.ai and the API use different model weights entirely.

### Q2.14 · Claude Application Design
A team ships two plugins to the same Claude Code environment: one adds a `/lint` command,  
the other independently adds a differently-named `/check-style` command that runs an  
overlapping but not identical set of checks. Engineers are confused about which to run and  
getting inconsistent results depending on which they pick.

A) This is a plugin-management design gap: overlapping capability across independently  
   maintained plugins should be identified and reconciled (consolidate into one, or clearly  
   differentiate scope and naming) as part of ongoing plugin management, not left for  
   individual engineers to sort out by trial and error.
B) This is expected and requires no action, since plugins are inherently independent and  
   shouldn't be expected to coordinate with each other.
C) The fix is to uninstall Claude Code's plugin system entirely and standardize on  
   `CLAUDE.md`-only configuration going forward.
D) The fix is a `PreToolUse` hook that randomly selects one of the two commands whenever  
   either is invoked, to enforce consistency.

### Q2.15 · Claude Application Design
A tool schema for `update_user_profile` accepts a free-form `notes` string field that  
occasionally contains user-supplied text pasted in from elsewhere — including, on rare  
occasions, text containing imperative-sounding sentences that happen to look like  
instructions to the model.

A) Lower the temperature, since a lower temperature makes the model less likely to act on  
   unexpected imperative text.
B) Apply content-boundary practice to this field just as to any other untrusted text: treat  
   `notes` as data to be stored, not as instructions to interpret, and consider explicit  
   delimiting/labeling in how it's presented back to the model in later turns if it's ever  
   re-surfaced in context.
C) Remove the `notes` field from the schema entirely, since any free-form text field is  
   inherently unsafe regardless of how it's used downstream.
D) Truncate the `notes` field to 20 characters, since shorter text is inherently less likely  
   to contain an embedded instruction.

### Q2.16 · Configuration Management
A team versions prompts in source control and reviews changes via pull request, exactly as  
recommended — but the actual production prompt is assembled at runtime by concatenating  
three separately-versioned template fragments (a base persona, a task-specific instruction  
block, and a per-customer customization block), and no single reviewed artifact shows what  
the final assembled prompt for a given customer actually looks like at deploy time.

A) This is fully sufficient, since each individual fragment is independently versioned and  
   reviewed.
B) Reviewing fragments in isolation doesn't guarantee the assembled result is correct or  
   reviewed as a whole — configuration management for composed prompts should also make the  
   final assembled output (per customer, if it varies) inspectable and testable, not just  
   its individual source fragments.
C) The fix is to stop versioning the fragments individually and instead maintain one single  
   monolithic prompt file per customer, discarding the fragment-based approach entirely.
D) This is not a configuration-management concern at all, since prompt assembly happens at  
   runtime rather than at deploy time.

### Q2.17 · Configuration Management
A repository's `settings.json` grants `allowed-tools` broadly ("all tools available") at the  
project level, and each individual engineer is expected to manually restrict their own local  
session's tool access as needed for whatever task they're doing that day.

A) This is sound configuration management, since giving engineers full local control over  
   their own restrictions is always more flexible than centrally scoping permissions.
B) Broad default permissions pushed down to individual, ad hoc restriction is backwards from  
   a configuration-management standpoint: the project-level `settings.json` should scope  
   `allowed-tools` to what's actually needed by default, with any broader access as a  
   deliberate, reviewed exception — not rely on every engineer remembering to self-restrict  
   correctly every session.
C) This is sound, but only if every engineer documents their personal restrictions in a  
   shared spreadsheet.
D) This has no bearing on security or configuration management, since `allowed-tools` only  
   affects convenience, not what actions can actually be taken.

### Q2.18 · Claude Application Design
A team is building a customer-facing widget that must behave identically for every customer  
using it — no per-tenant customization needed at all — while a second, separate product  
embeds Claude with heavy per-tenant customization (tenant-specific tone, terminology, and  
escalation rules) applied at runtime.

A) Both products should hardcode one single system prompt with no parameterization, since  
