## Domain 3: Claude Code (Q3.1–Q3.2)

### Q3.1 · Claude Code Operation
A team wants project-specific facts that Claude Code has already worked out during a session  
— like "this repo's test suite takes ~12 minutes to run" or "the staging deploy script lives  
in `scripts/deploy-staging.sh`" — to persist and be available in future sessions without  
re-discovering them each time, distinct from the standing conventions already documented in  
`CLAUDE.md`.

A) This is what Agent Memory is for: session-derived facts the agent has learned can be  
   persisted and recalled in later sessions, complementing (not duplicating) the standing,  
   human-authored conventions that live in `CLAUDE.md`.
B) This should also go in `CLAUDE.md`, since `CLAUDE.md` is the only mechanism Claude Code  
   has for retaining any information across sessions.
C) This is not possible in Claude Code — session-derived facts are always lost once a  
   session ends, regardless of mechanism.
D) This should be implemented as a custom slash command that the engineer must remember to  
   run manually at the start of every session to re-derive the same facts.

### Q3.2 · Claude Code Operation
A team wants a lightweight, reusable action ("run our standard PR checklist") available as a  
one-word invocation, and separately wants Claude Code to have durable, standing knowledge of  
the repo's architecture and conventions loaded automatically every session, without a  
command needing to be explicitly invoked.

A) Both belong in a custom slash command, since commands are Claude Code's only mechanism  
   for injecting any content into a session.
B) The checklist action fits a custom command (an explicit, on-demand invocation); the  
   standing architecture/convention knowledge fits `CLAUDE.md` (loaded automatically at  
   session start without being explicitly invoked) — different mechanisms for "invoked on  
   demand" versus "always present."
C) Both belong in `CLAUDE.md`, since anything the team wants Claude Code to know should be  
   loaded automatically regardless of whether it's needed every session.
D) Neither mechanism applies here; both requirements require a full MCP server  
   implementation.

