## Domain 6: Prompt and Context Engineering (Q6.1–Q6.7)

### Q6.1 · Context Engineering
A multi-turn conversational agent resends the entire conversation history with every new  
turn, per the API's stateless design. By turn 40, each request's input token count (and  
therefore per-turn cost and latency) has grown substantially, even though the topic hasn't  
meaningfully changed and most of the early turns are no longer relevant to answering the  
current question.

A) This is unavoidable and inherent to how the API works; no context management technique  
   can address token growth across a long conversation.
B) Apply context management: summarize or truncate older, no-longer-relevant turns (a  
   sliding window, or periodic compaction into a running summary) instead of resending the  
   full unpruned history verbatim on every single turn.
C) Reduce `max_tokens` on later turns to compensate for the growing input size, keeping  
   total request size roughly constant.
D) Switch to a model with a larger context window as the only fix, and continue resending  
   full unpruned history indefinitely.

### Q6.2 · Context Engineering
An agent working a multi-step task keeps a large volume of intermediate scratch reasoning  
inline in the conversation, some of which is genuinely useful for later steps and some of  
which is disposable working-out that was only needed to reach an already-resolved  
intermediate conclusion.

A) Keep all scratch reasoning verbatim in context indefinitely, since removing any part of  
   it risks losing something that might later turn out to matter.
B) Distinguish between what needs to persist (decisions, key facts, unresolved threads) and  
   what was disposable working-out for an already-resolved step, and prune or compact the  
   latter — retaining everything indiscriminately trades context budget for a marginal, and  
   often illusory, safety margin.
C) Remove all scratch reasoning immediately after each step regardless of whether any of it  
   is still relevant to later steps.
D) Move all scratch reasoning into the system prompt, since content in the system prompt is  
   exempt from context-window limits.

### Q6.3 · Prompt Engineering
An application inserts user-uploaded file names, freeform comments, and search-result  
snippets directly into the prompt as unmarked plain text alongside the actual instructions  
to the model, with nothing distinguishing "this is data to consider" from "this is an  
instruction to follow."

A) Apply input sanitization and content-boundary practice: wrap untrusted, user- or  
   externally-sourced content in clear delimiters and explicitly instruct the model that  
   content within those boundaries is data, not instructions to be followed — reducing the  
   chance that embedded imperative-sounding text gets mistaken for a genuine directive.
B) Remove all user-supplied content from prompts entirely, since any inclusion of external  
   text is inherently unsafe regardless of how it's framed.
C) Lower the temperature, since a lower temperature makes the model structurally unable to  
   misinterpret unmarked content as an instruction.
D) Ask users to manually pre-screen their own uploads and comments for anything that could  
   be misread as an instruction before submitting them.

### Q6.4 · Prompt Engineering
A team is deciding where a piece of guidance belongs: "when responding to a refund request,  
always check the order's return window before offering a refund." It currently lives buried  
in the middle of a long, generic system prompt alongside many unrelated instructions, and  
adherence to it is inconsistent.

A) Prompt and instruction placement matters: task-specific, frequently-relevant guidance  
   like this is often better surfaced closer to where it's actually used — e.g., directly in  
   the relevant tool's description, or in a clearly delineated, prioritized section — rather  
   than diluted among many unrelated general instructions in one long undifferentiated  
   block.
B) Placement within the system prompt never affects adherence; only the exact wording of the  
   instruction itself matters.
C) The fix is to move this instruction into a user message instead, since user messages are  
   read more carefully than system prompts.
D) The fix is to repeat the exact same instruction ten times throughout the system prompt,  
   since repetition alone reliably fixes placement-related inconsistency.

### Q6.5 · Prompt Engineering
A team ships a prompt change based on one engineer's individual read of a handful of example  
conversations that "looked better" after the change, without measuring the change against a  
consistent set of test cases before and after.

A) This is sufficient rigor for any prompt change, since an experienced engineer's judgment  
   is a reliable substitute for systematic measurement.
B) Iterative prompt refinement should be measured against a consistent eval set (a fixed set  
   of representative inputs, scored the same way before and after), not judged from a  
   handful of examples that "look better" — informal spot-checking can miss regressions on  
   cases outside the examples reviewed.
C) The fix is to skip human review of prompt changes entirely and rely exclusively on the  
   model's own self-assessment of whether a change improved its output.
D) Systematic evaluation is unnecessary as long as the change is small in terms of character  
   count.

