recommendations (which the company's compliance policy prohibits) is a single system-prompt  
line asking it to "avoid giving specific financial advice."

A) This is sufficient, since a clear system-prompt instruction reliably and completely  
   prevents the described category of output.
B) This is one layer among several that should exist: content-policy guardrails for a  
   compliance-sensitive domain like financial advice typically need output-side checks  
   (e.g., scanning responses for specific-recommendation patterns before they reach the  
   user) and monitoring, layered on top of — not instead of — the system-prompt guidance,  
   since prompt wording alone is a probabilistic, not enforceable, control.
C) This is unnecessary, since compliance policies around financial advice don't apply to  
